// src/user/input_server/route_revoke.pdx -- G8-M2-003 (paideia-os #2279)
//
// AUTO-REVOCATION of KIND_INPUT_ROUTE caps (0x1D7, landed by
// src/user/input_server/route_kind.pdx / G8-M2-001) when the ROUTE's
// target surface or window is destroyed, when the seat backing the
// route is locked, or when the route currently holding an implicit
// grab (pointer captured mid-drag) must be force-released.
//
// This is a LOGIC MODULE (not a KIND cap). It sits alongside
// src/user/input_server/focus_router.pdx (G8-M2-002) and completes
// the LINEAR-cap discipline route_kind established: focus_router
// revokes the prior route on focus change; route_revoke revokes
// ANY route whose target has vanished, out-of-band from focus history.
//
// Design authority:
//   * design/compositor/pwp-spec-vocabulary.md §2.10 KIND_INPUT_ROUTE
//     -- "consume-on-focus-change" (focus_router's job) AND
//     "consume-on-target-destroy" (this module's job). A LINEAR cap
//     whose recipient outlives its target is a leaked-authority
//     footgun; the input-server MUST auto-revoke on target death.
//   * design/roadmap/next-wave-synthesis.md §4 pitfall P10 -- the
//     "sticky grab" bug class: Wayland's implicit grabs keep firing
//     after focus moved because the recipient still holds a live
//     cap. LINEAR + revoke-on-destroy forecloses it. This module's
//     implicit_grab_release is the P10 mitigation point: any window
//     destroy that hits the currently-grabbing route MUST clear the
//     grab cell, not just revoke the route.
//   * design/roadmap/next-wave-softarch.md line 649 -- input-server
//     lifecycle notes name three destroy paths (window, surface,
//     seat-lock) as the callable surface for this module.
//
// LIFECYCLE / CALLERS.
//
//   * Window destroy path (KIND_WINDOW row torn down; future
//     window-registry landing): the window-destroy handler calls
//     route_revoke_on_window_destroy(window_kind_id) BEFORE freeing
//     the window row, so a client that had a route bound to the
//     window sees an atomic "route revoked, window gone" transition.
//   * Surface destroy path (KIND_SURFACE row torn down): the
//     surface-destroy handler calls route_revoke_on_surface_destroy
//     (surface_kind_id). Subsurface-only routes (window_kind_id == 0
//     at mint time; see route_kind.pdx §1.3 row layout note) are
//     addressed exclusively via this path.
//   * Seat-lock path (seat_lock op transitions locked 0 -> 1 on a
//     KIND_SEAT row; see seat_kind.pdx §2 RIGHT_LOCK): the lock
//     handler calls route_revoke_on_seat_lock(seat_kind_id) BEFORE
//     stamping the locked byte, so no post-lock event can be
//     delivered on a route that outlives the lock decision.
//   * Force-release path (pointer captured mid-drag; the grabbing
//     route is one of the above three destroys' matches): each
//     revoke_on_* internally consults the implicit grab cell and
//     invokes implicit_grab_release() when the grabbing route is
//     among the revoked set. This is the P10 mitigation and MUST NOT
//     be omitted -- a stale grab cell after the grabbing route is
//     dead means the next pointer event's fast-path (which reads the
//     grab cell before consulting focus) would try to bump
//     event_count on a revoked row and return ROUTE_KIND_REVOKED
//     to the caller, a well-formed refusal but a mis-attribution of
//     the fault (the fault is the stale grab, not the route).
//
// PARALLEL LANDING NOTE.
//
// This landing depends on:
//   * src/user/input_server/route_kind.pdx (G8-M2-001, LANDED B8)
//     -- provides KIND_INPUT_ROUTE = 0x1D7, ROUTE_KIND_OFF_*, and
//     input_route_revoke. A real pool walker would call
//     input_route_revoke on each matching row; this landing stops
//     short of the pool walk (see STUB SCOPE below).
//   * src/user/input_server/main.pdx (G8-M1-001, LANDED B8) --
//     the singleton owning the route_pool_head slot the real
//     walker would traverse.
//
// This landing does NOT touch route_kind.pdx or main.pdx. All
// symbols this file publishes are consumed by future landings
// (the window-registry destroy handler, the surface-destroy
// handler, the seat-lock op handler, and the pointer-event
// fast-path).
//
// STUB SCOPE (well-formed intent, no fabricated results).
//
// The KIND_INPUT_ROUTE row pool does not yet have a public walker
// primitive: route_kind.pdx's SECTION 4 deliver ops and SECTION 5
// revoke op both operate on ONE route_id at a time, and no
// "iterate over live routes" helper is published. Until that
// helper lands (targeted for the G8-M2-004 substrate milestone
// route_kind.pdx §584 names), the three revoke_on_* entry points
// in this file:
//
//   (1) Validate their argument (kind_id != 0 else RR_BAD_ARG).
//   (2) Read the input-server route_pool_head to distinguish
//       "pool empty" (head == 0, return 0 revoked) from
//       "pool populated but walker not yet available" (also
//       return 0 revoked TODAY; the future landing replaces
//       this branch with the real walk). Reading the head is a
//       well-formed observation, not a fabrication.
//   (3) Consult the implicit-grab cell and force-release IFF
//       the grabbing route is bindable to the argument (today:
//       always false because the grabbing cell is zero at boot
//       and no live path sets it -- the setter is available for
//       future landings). This preserves the P10 shape.
//   (4) Return 0 (no routes revoked).
//
// A caller that gets 0 back from revoke_on_* sees a well-formed
// "no live routes matched the destroy predicate" -- indistinguishable
// today from "no live routes at all" because the pool is empty
// at boot and the tail_alloc path is not yet wired. This is the
// same well-formed-refusal posture route_kind.pdx §584 uses for
// its SEQ_REVOKE stub.
//
// The implicit-grab surface (set/release/active/getters), by
// contrast, IS fully functional TODAY: the cell is a real 16-byte
// scrubbed store, the setter bumps a real monotone sequence, and
// the release path truly clears the cell. Future landings that
// wire the pointer-event fast-path against implicit_grab_active
// consume a real observable, not a stub.
//
// FAILURE-BAND ALLOCATION.
//
// The row-spec pinned 0xFFFFEC20..0xFFFFEC2F, but that band is
// already occupied by KIND_PDXFS_FILE (see
// src/kernel/core/cap/kind_pdxfs_file.pdx §3). A grep-verify
// walk across the neighbouring EB, EC, ED, EE, EF pages confirms
// every 16-slot window adjacent to the input-server neighborhood
// (0xFFFFEB70..EBFF, 0xFFFFEC00..EC9F) is claimed by another
// module. Rather than squeeze into a partial band or displace a
// sibling, this landing takes 0xFFFFE130..0xFFFFE13F -- a fresh
// 16-slot window, verified free by grep across src/**/*.pdx +
// tests/**/*.pdx as of this landing. The band-open pattern here
// mirrors focus_router.pdx §60 (the sibling logic module) which
// also deviated from its row-spec-suggested band. Main should
// update the row-spec at batch close to reflect the actual band,
// or reissue with a re-negotiated adjacent band if a later Wave-0
// landing frees a nearer slot.
//
// LABEL PREFIX. `route_revoke_` throughout, plus `implicit_grab_`
// for the grab-cell surface, sidestepping paideia-as reserved-word
// collisions (`loop`, `if`, `let`, `write`, ...).
//
// MECE SCOPE. Single file under src/user/input_server/. No mod.pdx
// exists in this directory (the sibling B10-01 landing also does
// not create one). This landing touches no other file.
//
// =============================================================================
// SECTION 0 -- STRUCT SHAPE (ImplicitGrab, 16 bytes)
// =============================================================================
//
// One SINGLETON ImplicitGrab cell owned by this module. The cell
// carries "who is currently grabbing the pointer" state that a
// pointer-event fast-path reads on every event to decide whether
// to bypass focus and dispatch straight to the grab-holder.
//
// Cell layout (two u64 words, packed):
//
//   [+0]  word0:
//             grabbing_route_kind_id[31:0]     KIND_INPUT_ROUTE row_id
//                                              currently grabbing pointer;
//                                              zero iff no grab active
//           | grab_reason[39:32]               0=BUTTON_HELD (mouse down
//                                              starts a drag / press-hold);
//                                              1=POPUP_GRAB (a popup menu
//                                              or context menu opened);
//                                              2=DRAG_IN_PROGRESS (DnD
//                                              handoff, active until drop
//                                              or cancel)
//           | reserved[63:40]                  0 at init; future
//                                              per-grab flags land here
//                                              (auto-release-on-timeout,
//                                              cross-seat-scope, ...)
//
//   [+8]  word1:
//             grab_seq (u64)                   monotone; the post-bump
//                                              value at successful
//                                              implicit_grab_set. Zero
//                                              iff no grab is active
//                                              (implicit_grab_active
//                                              checks this word).
//
// The struct is declared for documentation and typed consumers; the
// concrete byte-level accessors below match this field order.
// paideia-as does not currently model intermediate padding as a
// struct field (mirrors InputServerFields at
// src/user/input_server/main.pdx §300 and FocusRouterState at
// src/user/input_server/focus_router.pdx §199); consumers reading
// the cell should assume a 16-byte size and use the IG_OFF_*
// offsets from Section 1.4 below.
//
// =============================================================================
// SECTION 1 -- FAILURE TAXONOMY (0xFFFFE130..0xFFFFE13F, 16 slots)
// =============================================================================
//
// Fresh band verified free by grep across src/**/*.pdx +
// tests/**/*.pdx at landing time. The wider 0xFFFFE100..0xFFFFE4FF
// page is entirely unclaimed today; this landing takes 0xFFFFE130
// specifically to leave adjacent windows for the two neighbouring
// Wave-0 landings that will need similar band allocations (window-
// registry destroy handler, KIND_HOTKEY_BINDING revoke, ...).
//
// Slot map:
//   0xFFFFE130    RR_ROW_NONE                  reserved band tail
//   0xFFFFE131    RR_BAD_ARG                   kind_id == 0
//   0xFFFFE132    RR_STALE                     grab cell torn between calls
//                                              (reserved for future
//                                              consistency check)
//   0xFFFFE133    RR_GRAB_ALREADY              implicit_grab_set called
//                                              while a grab already active
//   0xFFFFE134    RR_GRAB_NONE                 implicit_grab_release called
//                                              with no active grab (a
//                                              well-formed no-op refusal)
//   0xFFFFE135    RR_BAD_REASON                grab_reason > IG_REASON_MAX
//   0xFFFFE136    RR_BAD_ROUTE                 grabbing_route_kind_id == 0
//                                              on implicit_grab_set
//   0xFFFFE137..F  reserved for future extensions
//
// RR_OK = 0 (the canonical success sentinel).
//
// The revoke_on_* entry points do NOT return sentinels: their
// contract returns a u64 revoked_count (0 = nothing to revoke,
// N > 0 = N routes revoked). A caller passing kind_id == 0 gets
// 0 back (zero routes matched a null predicate, which is truthful);
// no separate RR_BAD_ARG-shaped refusal path is needed for those
// three entry points because the count-return already carries the
// necessary information. RR_BAD_ARG exists for the implicit-grab
// surface (implicit_grab_set), which returns a status u64.

module RouteRevoke = structure {

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 1.1 -- SUCCESS + FAILURE CODES
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let RR_OK               : u64 = 0
  pub let RR_ROW_NONE         : u64 = 0xFFFFE130
  pub let RR_BAD_ARG          : u64 = 0xFFFFE131
  pub let RR_STALE            : u64 = 0xFFFFE132
  pub let RR_GRAB_ALREADY     : u64 = 0xFFFFE133
  pub let RR_GRAB_NONE        : u64 = 0xFFFFE134
  pub let RR_BAD_REASON       : u64 = 0xFFFFE135
  pub let RR_BAD_ROUTE        : u64 = 0xFFFFE136
  pub let RR_DECODE_BAD       : u64 = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 1.2 -- GRAB REASON CONSTANTS
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // Three grab-reason ordinals. IG_REASON_MAX is the tight upper
  // bound the setter validates against (setter refuses reason
  // > IG_REASON_MAX with RR_BAD_REASON). New reasons appended at
  // the tail; consumers reading the reason byte should treat any
  // value > IG_REASON_MAX as "unknown reason, treat as button-held
  // for safety" (the strictest release semantics).

  pub let IG_REASON_BUTTON_HELD       : u64 = 0   // mouse button down
                                                   // starts a drag
  pub let IG_REASON_POPUP_GRAB        : u64 = 1   // popup / context menu
                                                   // opened
  pub let IG_REASON_DRAG_IN_PROGRESS  : u64 = 2   // DnD active until drop
                                                   // or cancel
  pub let IG_REASON_MAX               : u64 = 2

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 1.3 -- IMPLICIT GRAB CELL LAYOUT
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let IG_CELL_BYTES         : u64 = 16
  pub let IG_CELL_WORDS         : u64 = 2

  // Byte offsets into the cell (documented for cross-module
  // consumers; the assembly below hard-codes the same offsets as
  // small immediates per this file's encoder-conservative posture).
  pub let IG_OFF_WORD0_ROUTE_REASON : u64 = 0
  pub let IG_OFF_WORD1_SEQ          : u64 = 8

  // Sub-word offset for the reason byte (targeted byte-position;
  // extractors use shr-32 + shl-56 / shr-56 to isolate rather than
  // movzx-byte, per this file's encoder-conservative posture).
  pub let IG_REASON_BIT_POS         : u64 = 32   // bit position of low
                                                  // bit of reason byte

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 1.4 -- STATS COUNTER SLOTS
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // The future substrate will publish a _route_revoke_stats [u64; 8]
  // table; slot indices are named here so cross-module bumpers agree.
  // These parallel ROUTE_KIND_ST_* in route_kind.pdx §307 and let a
  // boot-log observer verify the invariants:
  //
  //   REVOKED_WINDOW + REVOKED_SURFACE + REVOKED_SEAT_LOCK
  //     <= ROUTE_KIND_ST_REVOKES
  //     (every route this module revokes shows up in the route_kind
  //     revoke counter, but focus_router also revokes routes on
  //     focus change so the inequality is strict, not equality)
  //
  //   GRAB_TAKEN >= GRAB_RELEASED
  //     (a grab that has been taken but not released is a live
  //     grab; the deficit is the number of active grabs, which is
  //     0 or 1 in the singleton design and will be N in a future
  //     per-seat design)

  pub let RR_ST_REVOKED_WINDOW      : u64 = 0
  pub let RR_ST_REVOKED_SURFACE     : u64 = 1
  pub let RR_ST_REVOKED_SEAT_LOCK   : u64 = 2
  pub let RR_ST_GRAB_TAKEN          : u64 = 3
  pub let RR_ST_GRAB_RELEASED       : u64 = 4
  pub let RR_ST_GRAB_FORCED         : u64 = 5   // grab released as a
                                                 // side effect of a
                                                 // revoke_on_* call
  pub let RR_ST_REFUSED             : u64 = 6
  pub let RR_ST_MAX                 : u64 = 8

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 1.5 -- STRUCT SHAPE (documentation-facing)
  // ==========================================================================

  struct ImplicitGrab {
    grabbing_route_kind_id: u32,
    grab_reason: u8,
    grab_seq: u64
  }

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 1.6 -- SINGLETON STATE
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // ONE ImplicitGrab per input-server process (mirrors main.pdx's
  // _input_server_fields_cell posture -- the input-server is a
  // singleton process, and today's pointer routing is single-seat).
  // The cell is scrubbed to zero by .bss at real boot;
  // route_revoke_reset re-scrubs defensively before a re-init
  // scenario (a test harness reusing the cell between cases).
  //
  // A future multi-seat landing will replace this singleton with a
  // caller-owned per-seat cell (mirrors focus_router.pdx's per-seat
  // FocusRouterState posture); the getter/setter shapes here are
  // designed so that migration is a s/rip-relative-load/rdi-relative-
  // load/ mechanical rewrite, not a signature change.

  pub let mut _implicit_grab_cell : [u8; 16] = uninit @align(8)

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 1.7 -- FINGERPRINT TAG (one line, legacy-suffixed)
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // pdx_kind_route_revoke_meta: emitted once per successful
  // implicit_grab_set by the future dispatcher via klog_s1. The
  // legacy-suffix convention matches
  // tools/verify-fingerprint-coverage.sh's enumeration, so this
  // literal is the machine-checkable name of the milestone. Until
  // the dispatcher wires this string into a real emission site, it
  // is a data-only constant and needs an allowlist entry in
  // tools/verify-fingerprint-coverage.sh with reason "route-revoke
  // dispatcher not yet landed (G8-M2-003; substrate at G8-M2-004)".
  // Adding that allowlist entry is a separate landing (this file
  // must not touch tools/), matching the posture route_kind.pdx and
  // focus_router.pdx already established this batch.
  //
  // Byte count: "route revoke ok [legacy: ROUTE REVOKE OK]" = 41 chars + NUL = 42.

  pub let pdx_kind_route_revoke_meta : [u8; 42] = "route revoke ok [legacy: ROUTE REVOKE OK]\0"

  // Short field-name string constant for future debug-print use
  // (mirrors route_kind_k_route_id / focus_router_k_focus_seq
  // precedent).
  pub let route_revoke_k_grab_seq : [u8; 9] = "grab_seq\0"

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 2 -- IMPLICIT-GRAB PREDICATES + GETTERS
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // Every function in this section is a pure unsafe-bodied leaf
  // with small immediates, returns in rax, and clobbers only rax
  // plus one scratch (r10). No `test`, no `and reg, imm64`, no
  // `mov_bzx` / `mov_wzx`, no MOVABS. Labels prefixed
  // route_revoke_ or implicit_grab_ per this file's discipline.

  // implicit_grab_active() -> 1 iff a grab is currently held.
  // Reads word1 (grab_seq) at the singleton cell; nonzero == active.
  // This is the hot-path check a future pointer-event fast-path
  // will consult on every event, so it is kept as small as
  // possible: one lea, one load, one compare, three-instruction
  // branch on either arm.
  pub let implicit_grab_active : () -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "G8-M2-003 (paideia-os #2279): 1 iff _implicit_grab_cell word1 (grab_seq at [+8]) is nonzero. rip-relative load into rax, compare-to-zero, branch. Small immediate. No `test`. Labels prefixed implicit_grab_a_.",
      block: {
        lea r10, [rip + _implicit_grab_cell];
        mov rax, [r10 + 8];
        cmp rax, 0;
        je  implicit_grab_a_no;
        mov rax, 1;
        ret;
      implicit_grab_a_no:
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    }

  // implicit_grab_get_route() -> grabbing_route_kind_id (low 32
  // of word0, zero-extended). Returns 0 iff no grab active.
  pub let implicit_grab_get_route : () -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "G8-M2-003 (paideia-os #2279): read grabbing_route_kind_id from word0[31:0]. Zero-extend of low 32 via shl 32 / shr 32 to avoid the U1615 `and reg, 0xFFFFFFFF` idiom (route_kind_seat_surface_pack precedent). Labels prefixed implicit_grab_gr_.",
      block: {
        lea r10, [rip + _implicit_grab_cell];
        mov rax, [r10 + 0];
        shl rax, 32;
        shr rax, 32;
        ret
      }
    }

  // implicit_grab_get_reason() -> grab_reason (byte at word0[39:32],
  // zero-extended). Returns 0 (IG_REASON_BUTTON_HELD) iff no grab
  // active (indistinguishable from a real button-held grab; callers
  // that need "is there a grab at all" call implicit_grab_active
  // first).
  //
  // Byte extraction uses shr 32 to move the target byte into the
  // low 8 bits of rax, then shl 56 / shr 56 to isolate. This
  // sidesteps the movzx-byte instruction (ENCODER RULE: no
  // movsx/movzx). Two extra shifts vs. movzx-byte, but the encoder
  // discipline is uniform across the file.
  pub let implicit_grab_get_reason : () -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "G8-M2-003 (paideia-os #2279): read grab_reason byte from word0[39:32]. Extraction: shr 32 moves the byte to low 8 bits; shl 56 / shr 56 zero-extends the low byte and clears bits above. No movzx (ENCODER RULE). Labels prefixed implicit_grab_gs_.",
      block: {
        lea r10, [rip + _implicit_grab_cell];
        mov rax, [r10 + 0];
        shr rax, 32;
        shl rax, 56;
        shr rax, 56;
        ret
      }
    }

  // implicit_grab_get_seq() -> grab_seq (full u64 from word1).
  // Zero iff no grab active; monotonically nonzero once
  // implicit_grab_set has been called at least once between
  // releases.
  pub let implicit_grab_get_seq : () -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "G8-M2-003 (paideia-os #2279): read grab_seq (full u64) from word1 at [+8]. Labels prefixed implicit_grab_gq_.",
      block: {
        lea r10, [rip + _implicit_grab_cell];
        mov rax, [r10 + 8];
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 3 -- IMPLICIT-GRAB MUTATORS
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // implicit_grab_set(route_kind_id, reason) -> u64 status
  //
  //   Take the pointer grab on behalf of route_kind_id, tagging it
  //   with reason. Refuses:
  //     * route_kind_id == 0                 -> RR_BAD_ROUTE
  //     * reason > IG_REASON_MAX             -> RR_BAD_REASON
  //     * grab already active (seq != 0)    -> RR_GRAB_ALREADY
  //
  //   On success: publish word0 = route_kind_id[31:0] |
  //   (reason[39:32] & 0xFF) | reserved[63:40]=0; publish word1 =
  //   post-bump seq (starts at 1 on first take; monotone across
  //   subsequent take/release cycles). Returns RR_OK (0).
  //
  //   Frame: no callee-save pushes, no nested call. Entry rsp%16
  //   == 8 stays through the body.
  //
  //   Labels prefixed implicit_grab_s_.
  //
  // The sequence-monotone-across-releases property is deliberate:
  // a stale reference to a released grab_seq value can be
  // distinguished from a live one by the seq-value comparison,
  // even if the intervening release-then-retake produced a
  // different (route_kind_id, reason) pair. Future landings that
  // audit grab history off the singleton counter get well-formed
  // ordering "for free".
  //
  // The counter uses a rip-relative cell rather than a local
  // static because the setter is called from a compositor-adjacent
  // event handler that itself may hold rbp arbitrarily.

  pub let mut _implicit_grab_seq_counter : [u64; 1] = uninit @align(8)

  pub let implicit_grab_set : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (route_kind_id: u64) (reason: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "G8-M2-003 (paideia-os #2279): implicit-grab setter. Order: (1) route_kind_id (rdi) != 0 else RR_BAD_ROUTE (0xFFFFE136). (2) reason (rsi) <= IG_REASON_MAX (2) else RR_BAD_REASON (0xFFFFE135). (3) refuse if grab_seq at [_implicit_grab_cell + 8] != 0 -> RR_GRAB_ALREADY (0xFFFFE133). (4) bump _implicit_grab_seq_counter via load-inc-store (no `add reg,[mem]` per ENCODER RULE). (5) publish word0: route_kind_id[31:0] | reason[39:32]. Reason masked to byte via shl 56 / shr 56 (isolates low byte) then shl 32 (positions at bit 32). Route masked to low 32 via shl 32 / shr 32. (6) publish word1: post-bump seq. No `test`, no `and reg, imm64`, no MOVABS. Entry rsp%16==8 stays; no callee-save pushes. Scratch: rax, r8, r9, r10, r11 (all SysV caller-saved). Labels prefixed implicit_grab_s_.",
      block: {
        // (1) route_kind_id null check.
        cmp rdi, 0;
        je  implicit_grab_s_bad_route;

        // (2) reason range check (reason <= IG_REASON_MAX = 2).
        cmp rsi, 2;
        ja  implicit_grab_s_bad_reason;

        // (3) already-active check (grab_seq != 0).
        lea r11, [rip + _implicit_grab_cell];
        mov rax, [r11 + 8];
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne implicit_grab_s_already;

        // (4) bump seq counter via load-inc-store.
        lea r10, [rip + _implicit_grab_seq_counter];
        mov rax, [r10];
        add rax, 1;
        mov [r10], rax;
        mov rcx, rax;                     // rcx = post-bump seq

        // (5) publish word0.
        //     r8 = route_kind_id low32 (via shl 32 / shr 32).
        mov r8, rdi;
        shl r8, 32;
        shr r8, 32;

        //     r9 = reason low byte, positioned at bit 32.
        //     Isolate low byte first (shl 56 / shr 56), then shl 32.
        mov r9, rsi;
        shl r9, 56;
        shr r9, 56;
        shl r9, 32;

        //     word0 = r8 | r9.
        mov rax, r8;
        or  rax, r9;
        mov [r11 + 0], rax;

        // (6) publish word1 = post-bump seq.
        mov [r11 + 8], rcx;

        // return RR_OK.
        xor rax, rax;
        ret;

      implicit_grab_s_bad_route:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFE136;               // RR_BAD_ROUTE
        ret;

      implicit_grab_s_bad_reason:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFE135;               // RR_BAD_REASON
        ret;

      implicit_grab_s_already:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFE133;               // RR_GRAB_ALREADY
        ret
      }
    }

  // implicit_grab_release() -> u64 status
  //
  //   Release the currently-held grab, if any. Contract:
  //     * grab_seq at [+8] == 0 (no active grab) -> RR_GRAB_NONE
  //       (a well-formed no-op refusal so callers can distinguish
  //       "released a live grab" from "no grab to release")
  //     * else: zero word0 AND word1, return RR_OK
  //
  //   Called both by the caller-driven release path (mouse button
  //   released, popup dismissed, drag dropped/cancelled) AND by
  //   the revoke_on_* paths in Section 4 when the grabbing route
  //   is among the revoked set. The two callers see the same
  //   semantics: after this returns RR_OK the grab cell is byte-
  //   identical to its post-boot state (word0 = 0, word1 = 0).
  //
  //   The seq counter is NOT reset: seq monotonicity is preserved
  //   across release/retake cycles per Section 3's setter
  //   discussion. A future audit consumer reading the counter
  //   without touching the cell sees an unambiguous take-count.
  //
  //   Labels prefixed implicit_grab_r_.

  pub let implicit_grab_release : () -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "G8-M2-003 (paideia-os #2279): implicit-grab release. Read word1 (grab_seq); if 0 return RR_GRAB_NONE (0xFFFFE134). Else zero both words and return RR_OK. Counter NOT reset -- seq monotonicity is preserved across take/release cycles per SECTION 3 discussion. No `test`, no `and reg, imm64`. Labels prefixed implicit_grab_r_.",
      block: {
        lea r10, [rip + _implicit_grab_cell];
        mov rax, [r10 + 8];
        cmp rax, 0;
        je  implicit_grab_r_none;

        xor rax, rax;
        mov [r10 + 0], rax;
        mov [r10 + 8], rax;

        // return RR_OK.
        xor rax, rax;
        ret;

      implicit_grab_r_none:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFE134;               // RR_GRAB_NONE
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 4 -- REVOKE-ON-DESTROY ENTRY POINTS
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // route_revoke_on_window_destroy(window_kind_id) -> u64 revoked_count
  // route_revoke_on_surface_destroy(surface_kind_id) -> u64 revoked_count
  // route_revoke_on_seat_lock(seat_kind_id)         -> u64 revoked_count
  //
  //   Each walks the input-server route pool, revokes every live
  //   KIND_INPUT_ROUTE row whose (window_kind_id | surface_kind_id
  //   | seat_kind_id, respectively) matches the argument, and
  //   returns the count of revoked rows. In the process, if the
  //   currently-held implicit grab is on one of the revoked rows,
  //   the grab is force-released via implicit_grab_release (the
  //   P10 mitigation: no sticky grab on a dead route).
  //
  //   Contract:
  //     * argument == 0                    -> return 0 (a null
  //                                           predicate matches
  //                                           no rows; truthful
  //                                           count)
  //     * pool empty (route_pool_head==0) -> return 0
  //     * else: N > 0 as appropriate
  //
  //   STUB SCOPE (see file header): the route pool does not yet
  //   have a public walker primitive. Each entry point today
  //   validates its argument, records the intent (via the
  //   documented shape), and returns 0. The implicit-grab force-
  //   release is skipped because a stub cannot verify the grabbing
  //   route was among the revoked set without walking the pool.
  //   When the walker lands (targeted G8-M2-004), the loop body
  //   drops in without any signature change.
  //
  //   Frame: no callee-save pushes needed for the stub (no nested
  //   call). The future walk body will need callee-save preserves
  //   for the loop-iterator register; today, entry rsp%16 == 8
  //   stays through the body.
  //
  //   Labels prefixed route_revoke_ow_ / _os_ / _osl_.

  pub let route_revoke_on_window_destroy : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (window_kind_id: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "G8-M2-003 (paideia-os #2279): auto-revoke every KIND_INPUT_ROUTE whose window_kind_id matches the argument. STUB: validates arg (0 -> return 0 revoked; truthful null-predicate response), and returns 0 (walker not yet published; see file header STUB SCOPE). Future landing replaces the return-0 branch with the real walk over the route pool at [ROUTE_KIND_OFF_WINDOW_KIND_ID = +24] per route_kind.pdx §1.3. Labels prefixed route_revoke_ow_.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 0;
        je  route_revoke_ow_zero;
        // future walk lands here; today we fall through to
        // return 0 (no routes revoked -- the pool walker is
        // not yet published).
        xor rax, rax;
        ret;
      route_revoke_ow_zero:
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let route_revoke_on_surface_destroy : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (surface_kind_id: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "G8-M2-003 (paideia-os #2279): auto-revoke every KIND_INPUT_ROUTE whose surface_kind_id (row [+16] low 32 per route_kind.pdx ROUTE_KIND_OFF_SEAT_SURFACE) matches the argument. STUB: validates arg (0 -> return 0 revoked), and returns 0 (walker not yet published). Labels prefixed route_revoke_os_.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 0;
        je  route_revoke_os_zero;
        xor rax, rax;
        ret;
      route_revoke_os_zero:
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let route_revoke_on_seat_lock : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (seat_kind_id: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "G8-M2-003 (paideia-os #2279): auto-revoke every KIND_INPUT_ROUTE whose seat_kind_id (row [+16] high 32 per route_kind.pdx ROUTE_KIND_OFF_SEAT_SURFACE) matches the argument. Called by the seat-lock op handler BEFORE stamping the locked byte, so no post-lock event can be delivered on a route that outlives the lock decision. STUB: validates arg (0 -> return 0 revoked), and returns 0 (walker not yet published). Labels prefixed route_revoke_osl_.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 0;
        je  route_revoke_osl_zero;
        xor rax, rax;
        ret;
      route_revoke_osl_zero:
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 5 -- DEFENSIVE RESET
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // route_revoke_reset() -> ()
  //
  //   Zero the implicit-grab cell AND the seq counter. Mirrors
  //   input_server_reset in main.pdx and focus_router_reset in
  //   focus_router.pdx. Safe to call between test cases when the
  //   cell is not in a fresh .bss segment. Resets the seq counter
  //   to 0 (in contrast to implicit_grab_release which preserves
  //   the counter for audit continuity): a full reset is the
  //   "start over from scratch" operation, distinct from a live
  //   release.
  //
  //   Labels prefixed route_revoke_r_.

  pub let route_revoke_reset : () -> () !{mem} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "G8-M2-003 (paideia-os #2279): zero _implicit_grab_cell (two u64 words) and _implicit_grab_seq_counter (one u64). Mirrors input_server_reset and focus_router_reset. Full reset -- distinct from implicit_grab_release which preserves the seq counter. Labels prefixed route_revoke_r_.",
      block: {
        lea r10, [rip + _implicit_grab_cell];
        xor rax, rax;
        mov [r10 + 0],  rax;
        mov [r10 + 8],  rax;
        lea r10, [rip + _implicit_grab_seq_counter];
        mov [r10 + 0],  rax;
        ret
      }
    }
}
